(a) Except as otherwise provided in subsection (b) of this section, all burning within the incorporated limits of the City of Fort Oglethorpe is prohibited.
(b) Burning will be allowed, within the incorporated limits of the City of Fort Oglethorpe, if:
(1) The material to be burned cannot be removed by the waste disposal service employed within the incorporated limits of the City of Fort Oglethorpe;
(2) There is no other reasonable method to dispose of the material;
(3) The material is at least 50 feet from any structure; unless greater distance is imposed later in this code;
(4) An owner or occupant of the premises upon which the material is to be burned is present with a functioning fire control device for the duration of the burning activity;
(5) The material to be burned does not in any way consist of paper, cardboard, paperboard, treated wood, foam, lead paint, asbestos, rubber, plastics, tires, roofing materials (including shingles) sheetrock or gypsum board (or similar building materials), tar paper, petroleum products, or other material of a polluting nature; and
(6) All burning must conform to "Rules for Air Quality Control"; reference O.C.G.A., § 391-3-1-02(5)(a), (b) revised September, 1995, as follows.
(a) No person shall cause, suffer allow, or permit open burning in any area of the State except as follows:
1. Reduction of leavers on the premises on which they fall by the person in control of the premises, unless prohibited by local ordinance and/or regulation;
2. Carrying out recognized agricultural procedures necessary for production or harvesting of crops;
3. Destruction of combustible demolition or construction materials either on site or transported to a burning facility upon approval by the director, unless prohibited by local ordinance and/or regulation;
4. The burning over of any forest land by the owners of such land;
5. For recreational purposes or cooking food for immediate human consumption;
6. Fires set for purposes of training fire-fighting personnel when authorized by the appropriate governmental entity and the guidelines set forth by the director are strictly observed;
7. Disposal of tree limbs from storm damage;
8. For weed abatement, disease, and pest prevention;
9. Operation of devices using open flames such as tar kettle, blow torches, welding torches, portable heaters and other flame-making equipment;
10. Setting and maintenance by contractors and tradesmen of miscellaneous small fires necessary to such activities as streetpaving work installation or repair of utilities, provided that such fires are kept small in size, no smoke emissions exceeded 40 percent opacity, and that local ordinances and regulations do not prohibit such actions;
